# unit_tests.list — the single source of truth for the C unit-test suite.
#
# Consumed by BOTH .github/workflows/ci.yml (via `make -C tests check`) and
# tests/run_all.sh. Do not hand-maintain test lists anywhere else: TST-3 was
# exactly that drift (test_event_writer/test_event_reader missing from
# run_all.sh, test_concurrent_rw built but run nowhere).
#
# One C binary or Python test per line; '#' starts a comment. C binaries must
# be built by `make -C tests` (tests/Makefile TESTS).
test_wait_event
test_cmdline
test_spawn
test_bucket
test_event_writer
test_event_reader
test_trace_v2
test_sampler
test_anomaly
test_escalation_budget
test_exact_probe
test_effective_cores
test_backend_status_layout
test_synthetic_query
test_pgss_resolver
test_coop
test_concurrent_rw
test_durability
test_query_text
test_golden_fixture
test_live_accum
# Windowed aggregates (time model / top events / sessions / queries) must
# count only each event's in-window part, exactly as the AAS chart does --
# the cross-tab disagreement that failed a 35-minute rehearsal at 1.28%.
# Section 6 adds the RIGHT edge (#424): time is selected by overlap and
# clipped, occurrences are selected by the record's own close timestamp.
test_window_clip
# #424's overlap-admission unit test, landed on agent/424-historical with
# its tests/Makefile rule. Registered HERE because unit_tests.list is owned
# by the gate branch and this version is the one kept when the two branches
# are combined -- without the entry the binary is built by CI and run by
# nothing, which is the TST-3 false negative this list exists to prevent.
# NOTE: this branch must be COMBINED with agent/424-historical before any
# box-check or merge. Alone, the C tier exits 127 on a binary that does not
# exist yet -- correctly: a listed test that cannot run fails the gate.
test_window_overlap
# #586: the same edge problem on the LEFT. A record straddling `from` arrives
# whole, but the marker carrying its command's query id sits before `from` and
# is never loaded, so #128's rule 2 has nothing to resolve it with and the
# record reaches no per-query drill-down at all (183 of 184 records on the
# capture that filed it). The loader looks BACK a bounded distance for that
# marker and seeds the tag pass; it admits no record and widens no window,
# which is what sections 3 and 4 of the test pin.
test_left_edge_attrib
# #435/#436/#441: the four semantics contracts that are properties of the
# RESPONSE rather than of a compute result -- Overview's idle %DB as JSON null
# (not a numeric 0), the Queries latency average suppressed where no
# full-duration evidence exists, execution statistics suppressed under a pid
# drill they are not scoped to, and the transition graph's node population
# equal to its edge population under a filter.
test_semantics_wire
# #387: absence is distinct from measured zero, including records selected
# by end timestamp that have no in-window overlap.
test_time_model_absence
# #222 review round 3: a skipped EXEC_START (start past the caller's to_ns)
# must clear the pid's top_attributable bit, so a later wait event is never
# credited to an earlier, superseded still-open row. Unreachable through
# pgwt-server's own loader, hence a direct pgwt_compute_executions test.
test_executions_attribution
# #265: handle_top_queries' p95/p99 exchange sort (O(n^2), 10,000-sample
# arrays, 2338 ms per response) became qsort. The safety argument is that the
# output is bit-identical, so this is a differential against a verbatim copy
# of the deleted sort, plus the (int)(a-b) comparator trap and a mutation
# probe proving the differential can go red.
test_top_queries_percentile
# The loader's order restoration (src/event_sort.h): a fixed-width bottom-up
# mergesort that copied all n records ceil(log2 n) times became an in-place
# natural chain merge over the array's own runs. 29.88% of warm backend self
# time, 1779-1955 ms per request on a real 5.7M-event window, now 36 ms. The
# safety argument is bit-identical output, so the test is a differential
# against a verbatim copy of the deleted sort plus a mutation probe.
test_event_sort
# The SAME handler, the other 40% of its cost: handle_top_queries resolved
# each row's context (databaseid/userid/ambiguous) with a full scan of the
# window via query_context_for_events, and then scanned the window AGAIN for
# PGWT_EVENT_FLAG_QUERY_SYNTH -- 26 rows x 2 passes x 5.7M events, ~14 GB of
# memory traffic, measured at 1778 ms. Both facts are per-query_id properties
# of the same array and are now built in ONE pass (src/query_ctx_index.h).
# The whole safety argument is bit-identical output, so this is a
# differential against a VERBATIM copy of the deleted function plus the
# deleted synth loop, over four event ORDERS per fixture (the one-pass form
# is only equivalent because the rule is order-independent, which is
# asserted separately on the oracle itself). Seven mutants -- first-wins,
# latest-wins, resume-after-refusal, absent-reads-resolved,
# missing-metadata-ignored, zero-userid-accepted, synth-from-first-event-only
# -- must each be caught, and the deliberately BLIND fixtures (every pid
# resolves and agrees; no synth flag anywhere) must stay blind, so the
# suite's power is known to come from the adversarial fixtures.
# Bypass suite, because this table's failure mode is "no context", which is
# indistinguishable from a legitimately context-less row: a NULL resolver, a
# NULL/empty/negative range, an allocation failure at each of the two growth
# points, an injection point that is never reached, an unrelated injection
# name that must NOT fail, a partial range that must change the verdict, a
# comparator blind to one field, absent-vs-refused, and reuse after free.
# Non-vacuity ledger: fourteen counters, each failing the test at zero.
# Companion: tests/top_queries_context_mutations.py breaks one guard at a
# time (12 product + 12 test + 4 combos) and requires this test to notice
# each; the four COMBOS expect GREEN on purpose, because that is the only
# way to show a refusal gate is the UNIQUE detector of its defect rather
# than incidentally covered -- and the only way a weakened threshold
# (`> 0` -> `>= 0`) can be tested at all, since alone it stays green and
# proves nothing. `--self-test` drives the harness's own four failure modes
# (anchor matches nothing, mutation does not compile, and a mismatch in
# each direction) and requires all four to be REPORTED as failures. Not in
# this list because it recompiles the test 29 times (~20 s) and is
# evidence, not a gate. It earned its keep immediately: it found a
# redundant per-field slot initialisation that made the real (memset)
# initialisation untestable, and three mutations that were green because
# the shape they removed was still covered elsewhere.
test_top_queries_context
# #271 (correctness) + #269 (cost), the same four lines of
# pgwt_compute_variants: the p95 was selected from exec_times slots that the
# <= 10000 sampling guard never wrote (realloc does not zero), reported as a
# ~9.1-year p95 on the Transitions tab; and the sort was O(n^2). Now a
# written-slot counter plus qsort. Includes a differential against a verbatim
# copy of the deleted sort and a bypass suite (the issue's own
# identical-durations fixture is blind; injected realloc failures).
test_variants_p95
# #275: handle_top_queries kept per-pid lifecycle state in a fixed 512-entry
# array and pgwt_compute_variants in another, and both dropped the 513th
# distinct pid of the window SILENTLY -- short exec counts and percentiles
# under ordinary connection churn, with no flag anywhere. Both are now
# unbounded hash tables; the Queries-tab scan moved into compute.c so this
# test exercises the real code rather than a copy. Includes the bypass suite
# (assertion shapes and fixtures blind to the bug, pid 0, dense pid runs, and
# allocation failures that must come back ABSENT rather than short).
# Section 5 is #459: the same scan kept ONE open execution timestamp and ONE
# query id per pid, so an inner EXEC_START overwrote the outer start, the
# inner EXEC_END spent it, and the outer EXEC_END contributed nothing --
# Avg Exec reported the INNER statement's duration as the whole query's,
# while Waterfall (a per-pid STACK since #222) showed the right span beside
# it; a PLAN_START inside an execution re-keyed it to the planned query.
# Per-phase per-pid stacks now carry timestamp AND identity. Carries its own
# bypass suite: three mutant oracles (pre-fix single slot, shared query id,
# no CMD_END clear) that must each disagree with the real code, the FLAT
# fixture that is blind to all three, allocation-failure refusals on the new
# stacks, and a cross-check against pgwt_compute_executions with provenance
# that nested and inferred rows really existed.
test_pid_cap
# #363: pgwt_tag_events was `void` and could stop partway — a working
# allocation failure, or a window with more distinct pids than its per-pid
# table holds — leaving every record it never reached with the raw
# old_event/query_id it came off disk with, and returning as if it had
# finished. That array IS the oracle (#357: aggregates reconcile to it,
# never the reverse): the pass rewrites we==0 outside a command to
# NONCMD_CPU, which the contract EXCLUDES from DB Time, and back-fills
# query_id. So a partial pass reports more CPU attributed to the wrong
# statement — confidently wrong, not short (on the demo trace the relabel
# moves 44,119 records and one link by 16%). It now returns 1/0 and the
# loader routes 0 into its existing info->allocation_failed channel,
# discarding the array, because dump_summary reads that flag directly and a
# second flag would have left that route serving half-tagged records.
# Bypass suite, because "refuse always" satisfies every refusal assertion
# and "never refuse" satisfies every success one: the success path is pinned
# by literal tag counts, and an unknown injection name, an empty one, an
# injection armed but unreachable (count 0, or a counted point armed past
# this run's growths), a counted value that cannot be parsed (which must fail
# nothing, never everything), an empty window, a negative count, a fixture
# with nothing to tag, and leakage between cases each have their own case.
# #374 deleted the 4096-slot pid table, so the bound SEARCH that used to live
# in section 4 is gone with it (there is no threshold left to find); what
# replaced it asserts the removal positively (8192 pids tag in full) and
# covers the bound that is left — a real allocation failure, which can land
# mid-array and leave the refused array partly rewritten.
# Non-vacuity ledger: seven counters, each failing
# the test at zero. The call site is in server.c, which no C unit can link —
# tests/test_data_tag_refusal.py covers it through the real binary, on both
# the reject_overload and dump_summary routes.
test_tag_refusal
# #327: six open-addressing tables in compute.c paired a FIXED power-of-two
# array with a probe loop that had no fullness test, so the (size+1)-th
# distinct key spun forever -- pgwt-server is single-threaded, so that hangs
# every tab at once rather than returning a wrong number. The two summary
# sites are the dangerous pair: SUMMARY_MAX_SESSIONS/QUERIES bound ONE
# per-second record, not the window, and the summary paths never reach
# reject_overload, so the 900 s UI default had nothing bounding it. All six
# now grow (src/qid_index.h, src/pid_index.h) and refuse the whole result on
# a real allocation failure. Also #347's shape: an accumulator used as the
# occupancy test let a zero-duration record's slot be re-keyed and its
# transitions inherited by the next key (measured: 1 + 2 transitions
# reported as one row of 3). Pre-fix each section does not fail, it SPINS --
# the red evidence is non-termination under a timeout
# (tests/results/probe-bounds/redgreen-327.txt). Carries the bypass suite:
# cap-sized and identical-duration fixtures that are green while broken, a
# summary stub that delivers nothing or only part of the range, key 0, dense
# key runs, and allocation failures at every new growth point that must come
# back ABSENT rather than short.
test_probe_bounds
# #359: interference returns exactly at 4096 pairs and past the old cap,
# preserving every overlap; allocation failures refuse the whole result.
test_interference_bounds
# #276: pgwt_compute_concurrency kept candidate intervals in a fixed
# 100,000-entry array filled in ARRIVAL order, so the UI's default 900 s
# window drew its first ~60 s and reported the other 825 s as max: 0 -- 8% of
# the window, labelled "fidelity": "exact". Phase 1 is now bucketed on the fly
# with unbounded distinct-pid tables (no cap left to declare), bursts are the
# largest in each bucket with bursts_total declared, and the per-burst pid list
# is a declared sample. Carries a verbatim copy of the deleted code so the
# defect goes red on the same fixtures, plus the bypass suite (sub-cap and
# window-spanning fixtures that are GREEN while broken, nothing-qualifying
# windows, and allocation failures that must come back ABSENT).
test_concurrency_bounds
# A C unit test that tests/Makefile builds but this list never names is
# compiled, linked and then never run (TST-3). Nothing else checks that.
test_unit_list_coverage.py
# #202: the offline wait-CPU canary's decision logic (CPU recorded inside a
# pure sleep, cpu_ns > duration_ns) and every way it could be satisfied
# without looking at anything.
test_wait_cpu_canary
# Criteria item 2.5's raw per-class wait-CPU scan (tests/wait_cpu_scan.c) and
# its 29-case bypass suite. Registered here after review found the suite
# parked under tests/results/, where neither this list nor the registration
# guard could see it -- an instrument no runner builds is one nobody notices
# rotting, and this one's mutant anchor had already gone stale once. The tool
# and its fixture generator are built by tests/Makefile (wait_cpu_scan,
# wait_cpu_fixture); the suite REFUSES with exit 1 if either is missing
# rather than passing vacuously.
test_wait_cpu_scan.py
test_pg13_resolve
test_discovery_pie
test_discovery_nopie
# #464: PostgreSQL emits query__execute__start/done from TWO sites for
# plannable non-SELECT work, libbpf attaches USDT probes by NAME, and so
# every DML statement was counted twice (UPDATE exec 40 / plan 20 at N=20 on
# gate-1; SELECTs, which never reach the second site, 20/20). This covers the
# userspace half: the ELF note parser, the pure canonical-site selector, and
# the bypass suite for every way the selector could APPROVE without having
# seen anything (no notes, unresolvable PortalRun, a sizeless FUNC symbol, a
# truncated note descriptor, sites all outside the canonical range, half a
# pair, two canonical starts, an upstream refusal being cleared). Built twice
# (PIE / non-PIE) because an admitted site is only useful after the #24
# link-time-to-runtime translation.
test_exec_sites_pie
test_exec_sites_nopie
test_replay_fidelity
test_sampled_overhead_gate.py
test_data_query_text_context.py
